 In LaTeX, you can generate an automated table of Contents (ToC) using the `\tableofcontents` command, which is part of the standard LaTeX package `article.cls`. To ensure that the ToC is updated automatically whenever the document content changes, follow these steps:

1. Structure your document by using sections, subsections, and sub-subsections as needed. For this example, let's create a simple document with three levels of headings:

``` latex
\documentclass{article}

% Custom section and subsection titles
\title{My Automatic Table of Contents Example}
\author{Your Name}
\date{}

\usepackage[titles, title, and post-title]{tocbibind}

% Set the level depths for the automatic TOC
\setcounter{tocdepth}{3}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\section{Level 1: Sections}

\subsection{Level 2.1: Sub-Sections}

This text belongs to a sub-section.

\subsubsection{Level 3.1.1: Sub-Sub-Sections}

Here is the text for a sub-sub-section.

\end{document}
```

In this example, we set the `tocdepth` counter to 3, making all levels (sections, sub-sections, and sub-sub-sections) appear in the table of contents. The `tocbibind` package ensures that bibliography entries are also included when processing the ToC.

2. Compile your document at least once to create the initial ToC entries:

```
$ pdflatex my_example.tex
```

Your first compiled PDF should now contain a correctly generated ToC based on your LaTeX source.

3. Update the ToC automatically whenever you modify the source file:

After making edits or adding new sections, simply recompile the document with:

```
$ pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode my_example.tex
```

The nonstopmode option ensures that no user prompts are displayed during compilation, allowing the process to complete automatically. If any errors occur while compiling, LaTeX will stop at the error location and display an error message, requiring manual intervention to fix before continuing further.

By following the steps above, you'll have an automated table of contents that updates itself each time you compile your LaTeX document. This allows you to easily navigate through the various sections, sub-sections, and sub-sub-sections within your document. 

Keep in mind that some LaTeX packages and document classes might have different settings for generating ToCs, so make sure to consult their documentation if needed. Adjusting options like depths, formats, and inclusion/exclusion rules can vary depending on your specific use case.

 To check if a given number `n` is a power of three in Java, without using recursion or loops, we can take advantage of bit manipulation and logarithmic properties. A number `n` is a power of three if and only if the binary representation of `n` has exactly one "1" (a power of three) and all other digits are "0."

Here's the Java code:

```java
public static boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
    // If base case fails, the number isn't a power of 3
    if (n <= 0) return false;

    int bitIndex = 0;
    while (n > 1) {
        // Shift 'n' right by 1 bit
        n >>= 1;

        // Increment the exponent as 1-bit position found
        bitIndex++;
    }

    // Check for exact power of 3, if bitIndex equals logbase 3 (approximately 1)
    double logBaseThree = Math.log1p(1.0) * bitIndex;
    return (Math.pow(3, logBaseThree) == (double) n);
}
```

This algorithm calculates the approximate logbase 3 for a power of 3 using the log1p(1+x) property and then checks if it matches the given integer.

However, note that this method has limited precision due to floating-point arithmetic imprecisions. For larger powers, consider using a loop-based approach instead, or using a recursive method with some care taken to avoid stack overflow.

Reference:
- Log1P and its uses: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Log1p
- Logbase 3 vs. floating point log1p: https://math.stackexchange.com/questions/1340872/why-is-log1-1-not-equal-to-1-for-floating-point-numbers/1341553#1341553

